<% content_for :title, "Invite a user - #{current_organization.name}" %>

<%#
  Inviting someone to the organization, which is what this app means by adding a user: the person
  gets an invitation email and sets their own password. `UserInviteService` is the one place that
  happens, and the organization page's modal posts to the same action -- two entry points, one code
  path, so they cannot drift.

  It used to be a registration form. It asked for a name, an email, a **password** and a
  confirmation, and had no `create` action behind it: `essentials_form_for(@user)` inferred
  `users_path`, and `POST /users` resolves to `devise_invitable/registrations#create`. So the one
  link to this page -- "Add other users at your bank" on the dashboard -- led to a form that either
  registered an account outside the invitation flow or did nothing useful with it. The password
  fields are gone because an invited user chooses their own, and because the users table records an
  `invitation_status` that a directly-created account would never have.

  `form_tag` with flat params, not `form_for @user`: `invite_user` reads `params[:name]`,
  `params[:email]` and `params[:org]`, and matching it here is what keeps this on the existing
  action rather than adding a second one that does the same thing.
%>
<div class="px-4 py-6 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
  <%= render "shared/essentials/page_header",
        title: "Invite a user",
        subtitle: "They will get an email inviting them to #{current_organization.name}, and choose their own password.",
        back: {path: organization_path, label: "Back to organization"} %>

  <div class="max-w-2xl">
    <%= form_tag invite_user_organization_path do %>
      <%= render "shared/essentials/card",
            footer: capture {
              concat submit_tag("Send invitation", class: essentials_button_classes(variant: :primary),
                data: {disable_with: "Sending..."})
              concat essentials_link_button("Cancel", organization_path, variant: :ghost, size: :md)
            } do %>
        <div class="mb-4">
          <%= label_tag :name, "Name", class: "block text-sm font-medium text-slate-700" %>
          <%= text_field_tag :name, nil, required: true, autofocus: true, autocomplete: "off",
                class: "#{EssentialsUiHelper::FILTER_CONTROL_CLASSES} mt-1.5" %>
        </div>

        <div class="mb-4">
          <%= label_tag :email, "Email", class: "block text-sm font-medium text-slate-700" %>
          <%# `off`: this names a colleague, so the browser offering the signed-in user's own
              name and address would be exactly the wrong suggestion. %>
          <%= email_field_tag :email, nil, required: true, autocomplete: "off",
                class: "#{EssentialsUiHelper::FILTER_CONTROL_CLASSES} mt-1.5" %>
          <p class="mt-1 block text-xs text-slate-500">The invitation is sent here.</p>
        </div>

        <%= hidden_field_tag :org, current_organization.id %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
